Calibration: the lunar unit by these per-term constants (only demanded ops, trimmed cfgLow factors) is 0.65 CPU-h,
against 7.5-14.5 CPU-h for tier P (projected for the landed certificate, which evaluates every jet slot
and untrimmed cfgLow factors, SHARP-CERTSIDE): ratio 11-22, not resolved here.  The per-slot and GMP columns assume
the demanded-work design and may be optimistic by that ratio; the apps column is the landed one.

Shard limits in a 32 GB slice (29 GB to the kernel):
  landed packed product (fit 6.8e-4 S^1.28 GB): S <= 4141 terms (2-D), one declaration
  per-slot route: <= 3.5e+05 advice slots per declaration (factors pre-packed as data), 354 s kernel; model ops <= 1.3e+05 term-ops per declaration (166 s)
  GMP-resident: one whole product per declaration up to S = 1.8e+06 .. 3.6e+06 (L 59, beta 10), 986 .. 1972 s
  GMP-resident: one whole product per declaration up to S = 3.0e+06 .. 6.0e+06 (L 59, beta 6), 986 .. 1972 s
  GMP-resident: one whole product per declaration up to S = 2.7e+06 .. 5.5e+06 (L 34, beta 10), 986 .. 1972 s

Wall time = CPU-h / concurrent declarations.  Here: 124 GB allow 3 concurrent 32 GB declarations (about 12 at 8 GB);
a 1000-core cluster with 32 GB per core runs 1000.
